<title>RUSSIA: AGROKHLEB - Russian harvest seen down to 67-69 mln T.</title>
<headline>AGROKHLEB - Russian harvest seen down to 67-69 mln T.</headline>
<text>
<p>The following is a direct contribution from the Agrokhleb Report, published by SovEcon Ltd, an independent research organisation specialising in agricultural production and trade in the former Soviet Union.</p>
<p>RUSSIAN GRAIN HARVEST SEEN DOWN TO 67-69 MILLION TONNES</p>
<p>MOSCOW, Sept 30 - SovEcon has revised its forecast for Russia's total grain output in 1996 down by about two million tonnes to 67-69 million tonnes.</p>
<p>Due to summer drought, losses in grain plantings have amounted to nearly three million hectares, or 5.6 percent of total area sown to grain.</p>
<p>The drought particularly affected Rostov oblast (the Northern Caucasus), where losses in grain plantings accounted for about 20 percent of total grain plantings (Table 1).</p>
<p>In Western Siberia, the spring sowing campaign was delayed and harvest was slowed by rains in September.</p>
<p>The pace of the harvest is lagging by about two weeks -- by September 23, producers had threshed only 3.1 million hectares, or 49 percent of area threshed at the same date last year (Table 2).</p>
<p>As a result, nearly two thirds of grain plantings remain unthreshed.</p>
<p>This suggests that there will be additional losses due to delayed harvesting.</p>
<p>In Altai, the major grain producing region in Western Siberia, the situation is particularly difficult.</p>
<p>About one million hectares of crop plantings, mainly grains, were destroyed in September.</p>
<p>The region is likely to fail to meet its own grain requirements.</p>
<p>In Eastern Siberia, grain plantings were affected by summer drought and current yields are down 11 percent on last year.</p>
<p>The 1996 harvest will rise only seven percent on last year's drought-hit output and will constitute Russia's third-lowest output of grain in the last three decades (Table 3).  </p>
